<h2>The commercial relationship</h2>
<p>From 2015, the Delhi-based respondent supplied coating powder on credit to the appellant, a Rajasthan manufacturer. Part payments were made; a balance of ₹36,35,584 remained unpaid for supplies up to October 2018. Pre-institution mediation having failed, the supplier sued in January 2020. The buyer's defence ran on two rails: the Delhi court lacked territorial jurisdiction, and the goods — worth some ₹34.15 lakh — had been rejected for want of test reports and conformity with specifications.</p>

<h2>Jurisdiction: the debtor seeks the creditor</h2>
<p>The Bench held Delhi competent. In the absence of an agreement fixing another place of payment, the payment obligation was performable where the creditor was — Delhi — making part of the cause of action arise there under Section 20(c) of the CPC. The invoices, moreover, carried Delhi jurisdiction clauses. For suppliers, the combination is worth noting: the common-law rule supplies the principle, and consistent invoice clauses supply the paper. For buyers, the converse lesson is that a jurisdiction objection must be built on an actual agreement about the place of payment, not on the inconvenience of the forum.</p>

<h2>The rejection defence, dissected</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>No contractual foundation.</strong> The buyer could not prove that test reports were a contractual requirement, or that specifications had ever been agreed with the exactness the defence assumed.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No contemporaneous rejection.</strong> The record contained no letters, emails or debit notes rejecting the goods at the time of supply.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No return of goods.</strong> The allegedly rejected goods were never returned — retention and use pointing to acceptance under Section 42 of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930.</div>
</div>
<p>With the defence stripped away, the decree followed: the principal of ₹36.35 lakh, 18% interest pre-suit, and 12% pendente lite and future, with the balance payable within four weeks.</p>

<h2>Why quality defences fail so often</h2>
<p>Commercial courts see the rejection defence in a large share of supplier suits, and it fails in a recognisable pattern: the quality objection surfaces for the first time in the written statement, years after silent retention of the goods and part payments on account. The Sale of Goods Act gives buyers real rights — examination before acceptance, rejection of non-conforming goods, damages for breach of warranty — but each right has a temporal discipline. A buyer who intends to reject must say so when the goods arrive, in writing, and must not deal with them as owner thereafter. Once the goods are consumed, resold or simply kept without protest, the law treats the sale as accepted and remits the buyer to a warranty claim he must prove affirmatively.</p>

<h2>Checklist for credit suppliers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Print jurisdiction and interest clauses on every invoice, and keep proof of the buyer's receipt and non-objection.</p>
<p>Reconcile the account periodically and obtain balance confirmations — part payments and confirmations defeat later denials.</p>
<p>Complete pre-institution mediation under Section 12A before suing, unless urgent interim relief is genuinely needed.</p>
<p>Sue within limitation, computing it from each invoice or the acknowledged balance, whichever the record supports.</p>
</div>
